              %path{:d => "M 28.346781 -0.00009375 L 28.346781 19.52725 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "butt", "stroke-linejoin" => "miter", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.3985", :transform => "matrix(1, 0, 0, -1, 21.329, 77.246)"}
              %path{:d => "M 1.553689 2.070321 C 1.424782 1.296884 -0.00099875 0.128915 -0.387718 0.00000875 C -0.00099875 -0.128898 1.424782 -1.296866 1.553689 -2.070304 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "round", "stroke-linejoin" => "round", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.6376", :transform => "matrix(0, -1, -1, 0, 49.67579, 69.35447)"}
              %path{:d => "M 28.346781 37.163969 L 28.346781 56.695219 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "butt", "stroke-linejoin" => "miter", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.3985", :transform => "matrix(1, 0, 0, -1, 21.329, 77.246)"}
              %path{:d => "M 1.553221 2.070321 C 1.424315 1.296884 -0.00146625 0.128915 -0.388185 0.00000875 C -0.00146625 -0.128898 1.424315 -1.296866 1.553221 -2.070304 ", :fill => "none", :stroke => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "stroke-linecap" => "round", "stroke-linejoin" => "round", "stroke-miterlimit" => "10", "stroke-opacity" => "1", "stroke-width" => "0.6376", :transform => "matrix(0, -1, -1, 0, 49.67579, 32.18994)"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "3.902", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-2", :y => "50.577"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "11.347", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-1", :y => "52.37"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "83.271", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-2", :y => "50.577"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "90.717", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-2", :y => "52.37"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "46.152", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-3", :y => "12.204"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "46.658", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-4", :y => "89.996"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "29.81", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-5", :y => "36.403"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "36.462", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-1", :y => "38.197"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "58.156", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-5", :y => "36.403"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "64.808", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-2", :y => "38.197"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "30.282", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-6", :y => "64.484"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "35.99", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-1", :y => "66.277"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "58.629", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-0-6", :y => "64.484"}
              %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
                %use{:x => "64.336", "xlink:href" => "#HKZ0UV1gwnj8dbDBwy-bZ4S9XNQ=-glyph-1-2", :y => "66.277"}
          %p
            The vertical and horizontal arrows form categories (called
            = succeed ")," do
              %strong edge categories
          %p
            Vertical composition is given by the composition in the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_1 \stackrel{\to}{\to} D_0
            by virtue of it being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %p
            In particular, the
            %strong transpose
            of a double category, which switches the vertical and horizontal arrows, is again a double category.
          %p
            A double category is an important special case of an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+category"} n-fold category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category+of+squares"} double category of squares
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sq
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sq(C)
                whose objects are those of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                with their source and target both decomposed as a composite of two morphisms. (These squares are sometimes called
                %em quintets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi h
                      %mo ,
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\alpha,f,g,h,k)
                where
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi f
                        %mi g
                        %mo →
                        %mi h
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\colon f g \to h k
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quintet+construction"} quintet construction
              %p
                (In this example, the two edge categories coincide. Double categories with this property are called
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %strong edge-symmetric
            %li
              %p
                Since any 1-category can be regarded as a 2-category with only identity 2-cells, for any 1-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                we have a double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sq
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sq(C)
                whose squares are the commutative squares in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                squares.
            %li
              %p Any 2-category can also be made into a double category in two more ways, by defining the vertical or horizontal morphisms to consist only of identities. In this way 2-categories can be considered as a special case of double categories.
              %p
                In the other direction, any double category has two underlying 2-categories, consisting of the objects, the vertical (resp. horizontal) arrows, and the squares whose horizontal-arrow (resp. vertical-arrow) source and target are identities. We call these its
                %strong vertical 2-category
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong horizontal 2-category
            %li
              %p
                Finally, we can also make a 2-category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                into a double category with the same objects, whose horizontal arrows are the morphisms of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                = succeed "-pairs" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mate"} mate
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                There is a double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Prof
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
                whose objects are (small) categories, whose vertical arrows are functors, whose horizontal arrows are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equipment"} equipment
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            %li
              %p Any topological space has a “homotopy double groupoid” whose objects are points, whose morphisms of both types are paths (so it is edge-symmetric), and whose 2-cells are homotopies.
            %li
              %p
                There is a double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi MonCat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
                whose objects are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oplax+monoidal+functor"} oplax monoidal functor
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformation"} monoidal natural transformation
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category+of+algebras"} double category of algebras
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category+of+model+categories"} double category of model categories
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+functor"} Quillen functor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
                is a functor on this double category. More generally, we can define a double category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+categories"} homotopical categories
                and “left derivable” and “right derivable” functors.
          %h2#weakenings Weakenings
          %p
            An internal category in the 1-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            might more properly be called a
            %em strict
            double category, since all its composition operations are strictly associative and unital. Since a double category is a 2-dimensional structure, it makes sense to allow these compositions to be weak as well.
          %h3#pseudo_double_categories Pseudo double categories
          %p
            A
            %strong pseudo double category
            is a weakly internal category in the 2-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            is a “weakly enriched category.” This makes the composition in one direction weak, but the composition in the other direction remains strict (it is the composition in the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            that make up the pseudo double category). Many naturally occurring examples, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prof
          %p
            In pseudo double categories, the vertical and horizontal morphisms are often called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+and+loose+morphisms"} tight and loose morphisms
          %p
            Luckily, every pseudo double category is equivalent to a double category of the usual sort, where composition of arrows in both directions is strictly associative. This is Theorem 7.5 of Grandis and Paré‘s paper
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1999__40_3/CTGDC_1999__40_3_162_0/CTGDC_1999__40_3_162_0.pdf"} Limits in double categories
          %h3#double_bicategories Double bicategories
          %p
            One way to define a double category which is “weak in both directions” is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+bicategory"} double bicategory
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0611930"} Double bicategories and double cospans
          %p
            The definition of double bicategory takes as given two ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategories"} bicategories
          %p The reason for stipulating the vertical and horizontal bicategories as a basic part of the structure is that if identities are strict in neither direction, then the vertically (resp. horizontally) “globular” 2-cells (those whose vertical or horizontal boundaries are identities) can’t seemingly be composed vertically (resp. horizontally), so it’s hard to state coherence axioms for the associativity and unit constraints.
          %h3#strictly_unital_weak_double_categories Strictly unital weak double categories
          %p
            Another way around this difficulty is to require the
            %em units
            to be strict, but not the associativity. This is not such a terrible thing, since units can usually be strictified much more easily than associativity. Thus, in this approach we assume as given
          %ul
            %li a set of objects,
            %li two sets of vertical and horizontal arrows, equipped with identities and strictly unital (but not associative) composition operations,
            %li a set of squares, equipped with identities and strictly unital composition in both directions, and
            %li two sets of associativity squares, one with identity vertical boundary and one with identity horizontal boundary, satisfying the usual axioms, which are possible to state since identities are strict.
          %h3#cubical_bicategories Cubical bicategories
          %p
            Yet another approach to doubly-weak double categories was proposed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
            in an email to the categories list on 5 Mar 2010, in response to a query of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
          %blockquote
            %p
              A
              %strong cubical bicategory
              is given by sets of objects, of vertical arrows, of horizontal arrows and of squares, satisfying the obvious source and target criteria, together with operations of identity and binary composition for vertical and horizontal arrows, satisfying no laws at all; and finally, for every
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ×
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \times m
              grid of squares (where possibly
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              are zero), and every way of composing up the horizontal and vertical boundaries using the nullary and binary compositions, a composite square with those boundaries. The coherence axioms which this structure must satisfy say that any two ways of composing up a diagram of squares must give the same answer.
          %p He then commented:
          %blockquote
            %p I would be very interested to know if anyone can extract from this definition a finite collection of composition operations on squares, and a finite collection of equations between them, which together generate all the others. The key obstable seems to be problem that identity 1-cells are not strict in either direction.
          %p It also seems that a “cubical bicategory” in this sense need not have underlying horizontal or vertical bicategories, for the same reason. This presents something of an obstacle to practical applications.
          %h2#Unbiased Unbiased composition and associativity
          %p It is natural to ask what the “unbiased” notion of composition and associativity is for squares in a double category. The natural thing to take as input for an “unbiased composition” is a dissection of a rectangle into smaller rectangles, the smaller rectangles representing squares that we want to “compose up” in a double category. As usual, we can try to build up such a composite by means of the “biased” binary composites that are given in the definition of double category.
          %p
            It can be shown that if a given such dissection can be composed up in
            %em some
            way using binary composites, then any two ways to compose it up must give the same result. See
          %ul
            %li
              Dawson and Paré,
              %em General associativity and general composition for double categories
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1993__34_1_57_0/"} link
          %p
            Also of interest is “Characterizing tileorders” by the same authors, in which they characterize such rectangle-dissections, called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tileorders"} tileorders
          %p
            However, there are dissections which admit
            %em no
            composition in a general double category, foremost among which is the “pinwheel:”
          %p
            %img{:alt => "Pinwheel diagram in a double category", :src => "/nlab/files/double-category-pinwheel.png"}/
          %p It is shown in the paper
          %ul
            %li
              Dawson,
              %em A forbidden-suborder characterization of binarily-composable diagrams in double categories
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1995/n7/1-07abs.html"} TAC
          %p
            that this is the
            %em only obstacle
            in the same sense that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{3,3}
            are the only obstacles to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+graph"} planarity
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tileorder"} tileorder
          %p In many double categories, however, arbitrary arrangements of squares, even pinwheels, can be composed. By this we mean that given a pinwheel diagram in such a double category (for example), there is a natural square with the same boundary which it makes sense to regard as “the composite” of that pinwheel. For example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the double category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sq
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sq(C)
                of squares/quintets in a 2-category, the general pasting theorem for 2-categories shows that any arrangement of squares can be composed. In particular, this applies to commutative squares in a 1-category. The same is true for double categories of adjunctions.
            %li
              %p If all the squares in a pinwheel are pullback squares in a 1-category, so is the whole thing. The same is true for more general arrangements in a double category of pullback squares.
            %li
              %p
                Arbitrary arrangements can also be composed in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Model
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Model
                and its variants, again by the pasting theorem for 2-categories, since its 2-cells are just ordinary natural transformations.
            %li
              %p
                Pinwheels and more general arrangements can also be composed in the double category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi MonCat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mi Alg
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg
            %li
              %p
                In any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+double+category"} fibrant double category
          %p This last example is due to a factorization property: if we can factor the two long vertical rectangles in the pinwheel into two squares, then we can compose it. The cartesian cells in a fibrant double category certainly allow such a factorization. One can also axiomatize more precisely what factorization properties are necessary; this is done in the above paper of Dawson and Paré.
          %p
            In fact, it seems hard to find a naturally occurring double category in which pinwheels and more general arranguments
            %em cannot
            be composed, although such composability definitely does not follow from the double-category axioms. This suggests that perhaps the usual definition of double category is too naive, and we should actually require all “unbiased” composites to exist, including the pinwheel. There is probably a monad of some sort on a suitable category whose algebras are “double categories with generalized composites” in this sense.
          %p (It is tempting to try to axiomatize such a structure finitely by adding a basic “pinwheel-composition” operation, but although every non-composable arrangement reduces to one “containing” a pinwheel, the containment is not necessarily of a sort to which such an operation could be applied. For example, here is a configuration that cannot be composed even with a “pinwheel-composition” operation:
          %svg{:height => "59.926", :viewbox => "0 0 99.611 59.926", :width => "99.611", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
