          %p Thus, it is not clear whether there is any finitary axiomatization of such “augmented” double categories.)
          %h2#higher_categories_of_double_categories Higher categories of double categories
          %p
            There are notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+functor"} functor
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+transformation"} transformation
            between double categories, which make them into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
          %p
            Probably the most useful such 2-categories are where we choose one direction to be the “arrow” direction and the other the “proarrow” direction, we take functors which are lax, oplax, or pseudo in the proarrow direction and strict in the arrow direction, and transformations that go in the arrow direction. This is natural when considering double categories such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipments"} proarrow equipments
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
            in the arrow direction, we obtain a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            instead. In some situations, such as the study of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipments"} virtual equipments
            as well).
          %p
            We can also combine the horizontally lax and oplax functors, together with a general notion of vertical transformation, into a large double category of double categories. This is a special case of the double category of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            mentioned above; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category+of+algebras"} double category of algebras
          %p
            In some contexts, a natural observation is that the 1-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DblCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                locally double bicategory
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/locally+double+bicategory"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
          %p
            In any of these higher categories, we can apply the notions and methods of higher category theory. For instance, we automatically obtain a notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+double+category"} monoidal double category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonoid"} pseudomonoid
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DblCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            = succeed "," do
              %em cartesian double categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+objects"} cartesian objects
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DblCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DblCat
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+double+category"} fibrant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+bicategories"} cartesian bicategories
            can be obtained in this way.
          %p
            Finally, if we want to discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
            and colimits in double categories, or construct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
            based on double categories, we may want to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+equipped+with+proarrows"} equip DblCat with proarrows
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+profunctor"} double profunctor
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+equipment"} virtual equipment
          %h2#skew_closed_structures_on_the_category_of_double_categories (Skew) closed structures on the category of double categories
